ng the command to handle the -l flag and format output properly. I'm realizing I need to focus on just the implementation task for the long listing format without overthinking the PRD requirement numbering. The test suite structure shows there's a complete spec for the extended ls functionality, but I should propose the implementation work first and let the stitch agent handle the exact requirement mapping from the actual spec files. I'm settling on a task for ls-extended with the title \"prd008-ls R2: ls long format (-l) implementation\" since that's the most likely requirement group, and I'll reference the PRD for the stitch agent to fill in any gaps. Let me verify I'm not missing an obvious unimplemented requirement by checking what's already been covered across the existing packages—testutils through cat all have their core requirements implemented, so I need to see what's left in the remaining PRDs. Looking at the du implementation, it seems to only cover the core functionality without the common flags like `-a`, `-s`, `-h`, `-c`, and `-d` that would likely be in later requirements. Since du's release is marked complete, the code probably intentionally focuses on the essential use case. I'm going to go with ls-extended instead, which has a complete spec but no code yet. Given the constraints of four PRD sub-requirements and 250 lines, I should pick one major feature to implement rather than trying to cover everything.\n\nLong format (`-l`) is the most valuable but might exceed the line limit, so I'm weighing simpler options like recursive listing, sort flags, file type indicators, or inode numbers. Looking at the breakdown for `-l` specifically—permission formatting, user/group lookup, date formatting, and size handling—it's doable but tight, so I need to decide if that's the right scope or if something more modest makes more sense.\n\nI'm also realizing the PRD numbering might not follow the pattern I assumed. The code references R3.1 and R3.2 for hidden files