e sys and format packages provide, I can see they're designed to support extended ls features like color output, long format listings, time-based sorting, and human-readable file sizes. The --color flag seems like the most straightforward feature to implement given the existing infrastructure, so I'll focus on that.\n\nI'm going to add color support to the ls command by parsing the --color flag with its three modes (always/never/auto), then using the format package's color functions to colorize output based on file types. The ls main already imports the format package, so I just need to wire up the color methods alongside the existing column and padding logic. This should be around 50-100 lines of code and maps to the format package's color requirements. I need to be careful about the task constraints—I can only propose one task, so I should focus on just the implementation without tests. Looking at the required files and the PRD structure, I'm trying to identify which specific sub-requirements apply to the `--color` flag feature so I don't exceed the four sub-requirement limit per task. I'm realizing the color requirements for ls would actually live in prd008-ls rather than prd003-format, so I should reference that PRD directly and let the stitch agent resolve the exact requirement IDs. For the task itself, I'll focus on the implementation: parsing the --color flag with its three modes, calling the appropriate format functions to enable or disable colors, and using FileTypeColor() and Reset() to apply and terminate color sequences in the output. I'm realizing the color and display options might fall under R4, though I'm not entirely certain about the exact requirement mapping. Rather than guessing further on the requirement IDs, I could check the test suite documentation to see if it clarifies how the ls functionality is organized, which would give me more confidence in assigning the right IDs to this task. I'm weighing whether --color is actually in scope for this