e architecture tells me: pkg/format already provides ANSI color output utilities like FileTypeColor and Reset, and the existing code imports this package. So I can confidently base design decisions around using pkg/format for the color implementation, since that's architecture-derived rather than invented.\n\nNow I'm looking at the actual ls implementation to understand what changes are needed. The code already imports pkg/format and uses its column formatting functions, but doesn't currently use any color functions. The collectEntries function returns just file names as strings, so to add color support I'd need to extract file modes from the DirEntry objects—either through Type() or Info()—to determine which color to apply to each file.\n\nThe implementation plan involves modifying collectEntries to return both names and modes, then applying color wrapping in both the multi-column and single-column output functions. I'd also need to add a --color flag parser in main and a helper function to wrap entries with ANSI color codes. Looking at the scope, this should be around 60 lines of new code spread across flag parsing, the collectEntries refactor, and color application in the output functions.\n\nBut I'm realizing there's a complication: if I add ANSI escape codes to the entry names, the column width calculations in writeMultiColumn will break since those codes take up bytes but have zero display width. The current PadRight function uses rune-based width calculations, so it won't account for the invisible escape sequences. I need to either strip the codes before calculating widths, use a display-width function that ignores ANSI codes, or track both colored and uncolored versions of each name separately. This adds meaningful complexity to the task.\n\nThe cleanest approach is to compute the layout using uncolored names through format.Columns, then inject the color codes around those names during rendering while keeping the padding calculations based on the original un